old_sentence:
\subsection{Caputo-fractional heat conduction}
The Caputo derivative of order $\alpha>0$ with $m-1<\alpha<m$, $m$ integer, is \citep{Caputo1967,Podlubny1999}; at an integer order $\alpha=m$ it is defined as the ordinary derivative $f^{(m)}$, which is also the limit of the formula below
\begin{equation}
{}^C\!D^\alpha f(t)=\frac1{\Gamma(m-\alpha)}\int_0^t(t-\xi)^{m-\alpha-1}f^{(m)}(\xi)\,\dd\xi,\qquad
\mathcal L\{{}^C\!D^\alpha f\}=s^\alpha\hat f-\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}s^{\alpha-1-j}f^{(j)}(0).\label{eq:caputo}
\end{equation}
The Laplace rule in \eqref{eq:caputo} is the reason the Caputo definition is favored: initial conditions keep their usual physical meaning.

